    test(ts): pin the by-name runtime import the package shell rejection test asserts against
    
    The by-name test of feature spec 24 inherited its runtime import from
    examples/ts.hxml: when that file carried -D runtime-import=@boring/runtime
    the test dropped the repository's opt-out and the combination (by-name
    import + emitted manifest) aborted, as spec 24 Ruling 5 requires. The
    bundle-driver commit (2bd609b9) moved the repository hxml to the relative
    specifier ./runtime without updating the test, so the scenario silently
    became relative import + emitted manifest - which the same ruling
    requires to be accepted - and the expectation started failing
    deterministically against a correct compiler (exit 0, valid manifest).
    
    The guard at tscompiler/Compiler.hx (emitPackageShell) enforces exactly
    the spec's scope: it runs only while the shell is enabled and stops a
    compilation that uses the runtime through a non-relative import. No
    compiler or spec change is warranted. The test now pins
    runtime-import=@boring/runtime itself, so it exercises the by-name
    rejection regardless of the repository hxml state; rewriteHxml rewrites
    whatever specifier the hxml currently carries instead of matching only
    the historical value. Assertions are unchanged.
    
    Also supersedes the BASELINE-FAILURES.md entry that read this failure as
    a product/spec gap: it was a stale test expectation.

